Input: {"question": "What is the profession of Richard Sheppard?", "source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d00", "start": 0, "end": 170, "text": "Dick Sheppard (priest) Hugh Richard Lawrie \"Dick\" Sheppard (2 September 1880 – 31 October 1937) was an English Anglican priest, Dean of Canterbury and Christian pacifist."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d00", "start": 171, "end": 294, "text": "Sheppard was the younger son of Edgar Sheppard, a minor canon at the Royal Chapel of All Saints in Windsor, and Mary White."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d00", "start": 295, "end": 413, "text": "Born at the Cloisters in Windsor, he was educated at Marlborough College and then (1901–1904) Trinity Hall, Cambridge."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d00", "start": 414, "end": 542, "text": "He worked with the poor from Oxford House, Bethnal Green and then for a year as secretary to Cosmo Lang, then Bishop of Stepney."}, "D1S5": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d00", "start": 543, "end": 590, "text": "He volunteered to serve in the Second Boer War:"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d01", "start": 0, "end": 164, "text": "what sort of man this Anglican priest was who had convinced Huxley not only to take up pacifism but to recognize that it required a spiritual dimension to succeed ."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d01", "start": 165, "end": 464, "text": "I soon realized that Sheppard and his Peace Pledge Union had been reduced to a footnote by literary historians of the period , such as Valentine Cunningham , who wrote in British Writers of the Thirties that the Peace Pledge Union was “ really such a non-starter among ’ 30s intellectuals ” ( 69 ) ."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0282-111de4af9a55:d01", "